  "segments": [
    {
      "text": "Daksha, the son of Brahma, lost 6,000 sons, every single one, and the man who took them never raised a hand. Daksha had 5,000 sons called the Haryashvas. He told them to rule the earth and have children. They agreed. Before they set out, they gathered where the River Sindhu meets the sea to make offerings. Narada, the divine sage, was on his way to them. He asked how they could rule a land they knew nothing about. How would they know if it had too few people or too many? The brothers had no answer. Narada smirked. He told them to learn every detail about the earth first. They might even find a higher path. Doubt crept over 5,000 faces. The brothers left to explore the earth. No one saw them again. They never came back. Daksha and his wife Ashikli had another thousand sons, the Shavalashvas. Narada spoke to them in exactly the same way. They vanished too. Daksha roared, \"Who would build homes if everyone became a hermit?\" Narada said he had only shown them a path. It was their choice to walk it. Daksha cursed him. Narada had scattered his sons across the earth, so he would wander the three worlds forever and never find a home. Was Narada a guide or a thief of sons?",
      "start_time": 0.16,
      "end_time": 76.38,
